# [[ project_name ]]

MicroPython firmware for the [[ target_port ]] port[% if target_port != "unix" %], board `[[ project_board_name ]]`[% endif %].

## Building

Builds run inside the container mpbuild selects for this port. The Makefile
detects when it is already inside a container or in CI and builds directly.

```bash
make submodules     # fetch MicroPython and the libraries
make system         # build the firmware with the application frozen in
make libs-only      # build with only the libraries frozen, no application
[% if use_mboot %]make mboot          # build the bootloader
make deploy-dfu     # flash over DFU
[% endif %]make clean          # remove build output
```

Firmware lands in `src/system/build-[[ project_board_name ]]/`.

The container names live in `src/system/build_containers.mk`. Re-run
`make setup` after moving the MicroPython submodule so they stay in step; the
esp32 image in particular is derived from that tree's IDF lockfile.

## Testing

```bash
make tests       # unit tests, on the unix port
make checks      # ruff, mypy, yamllint via pre-commit
make repl-unix   # REPL on the unix port with simulated hardware
make run-unix    # run the application on the unix port
```

`src/unix/simulation` carries stand-ins for the hardware modules the
application imports. It covers only what the application touches, so extend it
alongside `hardware.py`, and keep anything needing real peripheral behaviour in
an on-target test.

## Layout

```
src/firmware/application  frozen application code
src/firmware/test         unit tests, run on the unix port
src/libs                  library submodules frozen into the firmware
src/micropython           MicroPython (submodule)
src/system                board definition and build output
src/unix                  unix port build and hardware simulation
tools                     build helpers and project setup
```

The application is six modules: `boot.py` prepares the filesystem, `main.py`
starts the application, `device.py` owns the event loop and task list,
`hardware.py` constructs everything that touches a peripheral,
`device_config.py` holds settings, and `version.py` reports the build.

## Versioning

The version is `git describe` output against tags matching `v[0-9]*`, so
`v1.2.0` on a tagged commit and `v1.2.0-4-gabc1234` four commits later. Tag a
commit to make a release build.

It is resolved on the host and passed into the build through
`MICROPY_GIT_TAG` / `MICROPY_GIT_HASH`, which MicroPython's own
`makeversionhdr.py` prefers over its internal lookups. One mechanism covers the
make-based ports and the cmake ones alike, and git stays out of the build
container.

```python
import version
print(version.firmware_version)
[% if use_mboot %]print(version.bootloader_version)
[% endif %]```

## Type checking

Stubs are installed into `tools/typings` by `make typings` and used as a custom
typeshed by both mypy and Pylance. `src/unix/simulation` is deliberately absent
from the type paths, so `machine` resolves to the port stubs rather than the
simulation shims.
